<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sylhet</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sylhet is a city in north-eastern Bangladesh on the Surma river and the centre of Sylhet Division. Conquered by Muslim forces from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> in 1303, it is known for the shrine of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> saint Shah Jalal, and under British rule it became a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> growing. In 1947 a referendum joined most of the district to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a>, and many Sylhetis later emigrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sylhet.jpg" alt="Sylhet" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rocky Masum; This work was made by Masum-al-Hasan Rocky and released under the license(s) stated below. … · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sylhet_city_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sylhet lies on the banks of the Surma River in north-eastern Bangladesh, in Sylhet Division. It has a tropical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> climate, with a rainy season from April to October; nearly 80 percent of the average annual rainfall of 4,200 millimetres falls between May and September. The surrounding region is geologically complex, and limestone deposits suggest that the whole area lay under the ocean in the Oligo-Miocene. Three major earthquakes of at least magnitude 7.5 have struck the city in the last 150 years, the most recent in 1918. The name is an anglicisation of Śilhôṭ, thought to derive from the Sanskrit Śrīhaṭṭa, which takes its name from Śrīhaṭṭanātha, the tutelary deity of the Nātha dynasty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The region has been inhabited since ancient times. In 1303 Shamsuddin Firoz Shah, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaur">Lakhnauti</a>, conquered Sylhet by defeating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> ruler Gour Govinda, and it became part of the Bengal Sultanate. In the 16th century the Baro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhuyan">Bhuiyan</a> zamindars, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardar">Sardars</a> of the Pathan Confederacy, controlled it. It later became a sarkar, or district, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>, and after 1612 it was the Mughals' most significant imperial outpost in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhaka">Dhaka</a>, a position it kept throughout the 17th century.</p><p>British rule began in the 18th century under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>. Sylhet became a key source of lascars for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>, in part because of its ancient seafaring tradition. Robert Lindsay, District Collector from 1778 to 1790, described the town as merely a small market, with houses on hills hidden behind forests. The municipal board was established in 1867, and in 1874 the town became part of Colonial Assam.</p><p>An earthquake on 12 June 1897 demolished almost the whole town, and a modern new town on a European model was built on the wreckage. From the beginning of the 20th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> industry increased its importance. In 1947, following a referendum and the partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, Sylhet became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a>. In 1995 Bangladesh declared it the sixth divisional headquarters of the country.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The 2022 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 532,839 people in the city. Most are Muslims, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> minority of about 9 percent, and Sylheti is spoken by the vast majority. The Shah Jalal Dargah, an Islamic shrine, draws thousands of pilgrims a year. The hinterland holds the country's largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> plantations and natural gas fields, and the city's economy is closely linked with the Bangladeshi diaspora, especially the British Bangladeshi community, whose remittances have driven growth in construction. Shahjalal University of Science and Technology and Sylhet Agricultural University are the public universities.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sylhet” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Jaintia kingdom grew from chiefdoms of the Pnar (Synteng) people of the eastern Meghalaya plateau, who extended their rule into the plains north of <b>Sylhet</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/jaintia-kingdom">Jaintia Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Its villages lay in hilly, forested country above the <b>Sylhet</b> plains.</blockquote><a href="/state/langrin-state">Langrin State</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1820s Syiem U Tirot Sing agreed to let the British build a road from Assam to <b>Sylhet</b> through his territory.</blockquote><a href="/state/nongkhlaw-state">Nongkhlaw State</a></li><li><blockquote>Hima Sohra was one of the Khasi chiefdoms on the southern edge of the Meghalaya plateau, above the plains of <b>Sylhet</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/sohra-state">Sohra State</a></li></ul></section>
